#~
Try() -- and '?->', which is Try() on the chain -- recovers when a COMPILED
method calls a COMPILED method that fails. That is gap 3 of #925, the last of
the three, and the one the cast work did not reach.

A divide by zero is used deliberately. It is raised by a guard stub inside the
callee, so the status travels the native call site (EmitNativeCallSite) rather
than the bridge callback the cast half uses -- a different path out of compiled
code, and the one that used to call JitNativeCallError and exit.

THE WARM-UP IS PART OF THE TEST, twice over. Hop is called repeatedly with a
divisor that works, so that BOTH Hop and the Ratio it calls compile; only then
is the chain given a zero. Without it, neither is compiled, the inner call goes
through the bridge, and this test would cover the interpreted path while looking
like it covered the compiled one. The method holding the '?->' stays interpreted
either way, because TRY_START is in neither backend's whitelist, so the handler
always lives in an interpreted frame.

WHAT FAILS WITHOUT THE FIX, measured rather than assumed: this test segfaults
(exit 139, output lost with the buffer), and the unguarded form prints a stack
listing that names Main at pos=3. Both come from frame bookkeeping, not from the
propagation: EmitNativePrologue pushes a frame record and the only matching
decrement was in the RTRN handler, which a guard stub never reaches, so a
natively entered method that failed left its record registered. Recovery then
walked entries that are not frames. The pop now lives at the head of the
teardown, the one point every exit reaches.

The repeated-recovery loop is there for a future half-fix. One recovery is
enough to crash the pre-fix build, but a pop that is off by one, or that misses
one of the two frames in the chain, would drift CALL_STACK_SIZE (256) slots and
only show after many failures.

The cast half of #925 lives in try_recovers_cast.obs and the recursion half of
#900 in try_recovers_depth.obs, which still opts out of the JIT pass.
~#

class Deep {
	@n : Int;
	@inner : Deep;

	New(n : Int, inner : Deep) {
		@n := n;
		@inner := inner;
	}

	# compiled, and the call to Ratio is a native-to-native call once both are
	method : public : Hop() ~ Deep {
		if(@inner->Ratio() > 0) {
			return @self;
		};

		return @self;
	}

	method : public : Ratio() ~ Int {
		return 10 / @n;
	}
}

class TryRecoversJitCall {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		ok := true;

		# Warm Hop AND Ratio past the JIT threshold with a divisor that works,
		# so the failing call below runs compiled-calling-compiled every pass.
		good := Deep->New(1, Deep->New(5, Nil));
		warm := true;
		each(i : 500) {
			if(good?->Hop() = Nil) {
				warm := false;
			};
		};
		ok := Report("the warm chain returns its object", warm) & ok;

		# the callee now divides by zero, deep inside the compiled chain
		bad := Deep->New(1, Deep->New(0, Nil));
		hopped := bad?->Hop();
		ok := Report("a failing compiled callee is recovered, not fatal", hopped = Nil) & ok;

		# and the program carries on, with the recovered frames not in the way
		ok := Report("a good chain after recovery still works", good?->Hop() <> Nil) & ok;

		# Recover far more times than the call stack has slots (256), so a pop
		# that is off by one cannot hide behind a single failure.
		#
		# The result is ASSIGNED to a local and the local compared, to keep this
		# test about #925 alone. Comparing a recovered '?->' in place is the
		# property #1067 covers, in try_chain_inline_compare.obs; it was broken
		# until #1071 and is correct now, but pulling it in here would mean a
		# failure in either one showing up as a failure in both.
		drift := true;
		hop_result : Deep;
		each(i : 300) {
			hop_result := bad?->Hop();
			if(hop_result <> Nil) {
				drift := false;
			};
		};
		ok := Report("300 recoveries do not drift the call stack", drift) & ok;
		ok := Report("the good chain still works after all of them", good?->Hop() <> Nil) & ok;

		if(<>ok) {
			Runtime->Exit(1);
		};
	}

	function : Report(name : String, ok : Bool) ~ Bool {
		if(ok) {
			"PASS: {$name}"->PrintLine();
			return true;
		};

		"FAIL: {$name}"->PrintLine();
		return false;
	}
}
